  "name": "Low End",
  "description": "A rock band, done properly: Unreal Instruments' METAL-GTX, a DI guitar double-tracked through an amp that lives inside its SFZ and a real 4x12 of Vintage 30s, miked with an SM57 in two different places for the two takes (impulse responses from Jester Dyne's CC0 Brutal Pack); Unreal Instruments' Standard Bass, a DI-recorded five-string with 1,400 samples, eighteen alternating round robins and keyswitched articulations, put through an amp that lives inside its own SFZ; and SM Drums, a public-domain rock kit with up to 48 velocity layers and eight round robins a piece, split into five pieces that each have their own EQ and compression. Over them, three synths sfizz generates itself with no samples at all: Veil, a dark supersaw pad with an open-vowel formant inside it that carries the thirds and sevenths the power chords leave out, Glass, a filter pluck with a struck-bar bell under it for the arpeggios; and Wire, a singing lead of narrow detuned saws over a square an octave down, with a vibrato the score opens on held notes, for the hook.",
